<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">San José, Costa Rica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San José is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>, in the Central Valley. Founded in the 18th century, it became capital in 1823 after a brief civil war in which its republicans defeated the conservative towns of Cartago and Heredia over joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It grew rich from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> in the 19th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-union-1921">Pact of Union of Central America</a> was signed there in 1921.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-jose-costa-rica.jpg" alt="San José, Costa Rica" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alejandro Álvarez Taborda (Aleat88) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciudad_de_San_Jos%C3%A9.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>San José stands in the middle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>, in the mid-west of the Central Valley. It is the seat of national government, the focus of political and economic activity and the country's chief transport hub. Together with the neighbouring cantons of Alajuela, Heredia and Cartago it forms the Greater Metropolitan Area. The city is named in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-joseph">Joseph of Nazareth</a>. Its climate is tropical wet and dry, with average temperatures that vary little through the year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>San José was founded in 1736 by order of the Cabildo de León, to gather the scattered inhabitants of the Aserrí Valley. A chapel was completed a year later with help from the church of Cartago, and St Joseph was chosen as parish patron, which gave the town its name. Unlike Cartago, it was not founded by formal decree and so lacked a city government until the Constitution of Cádiz was enacted in 1812. On 18 October 1813 the presbyter Florencio del Castillo defined it as a city, but the title was lost in 1814 when Ferdinand VII annulled the proceedings of the courts. It was restored in 1820, and in 1823 San José became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>.</p><p>The installation of the Tobacco Factory in 1782 spurred growth, and capital from tobacco plantations let the city surpass neighbouring provinces economically. In the late 19th century the French coffee entrepreneur Monsieur Amon created the first modern urban neighbourhood, Barrio Amon, in Belle Époque style; it and the National Theater remain symbols of the Costa Rican coffee golden age. The University of Santo Tomas, the country's first university, was established in San José in 1843 and closed in 1888.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>San José is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>, with an estimated 352,381 people in its canton in 2022. It holds the National Museum of Costa Rica and the National Theater, considered the finest historic building in the capital; El Teatro Variedades, built in 1892, is its oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. The Museum of Pre-Columbian Gold and the Museum of Jade are also in the city. The University for Peace, established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> in 1980, is located in San José.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “San José, Costa Rica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For the hundredth anniversary of independence, delegates of the five Central American states signed a Pact of Union at <b>San José</b> in January 1921.</blockquote><a href="/state/federation-of-central-america-1921-1922">Federation of Central America (1921-1922)</a></li><li><blockquote>Costa Rica joined the Federal Republic of Central America in 1824 after rivalry among its towns over joining Mexico had been settled by a brief civil war in 1823, after which the capital moved from Cartago to <b>San José</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-costa-rica">State of Costa Rica</a></li></ul></section>
